WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.762
Chrome has to keep three billion people away from malicious websites.

00:00:05.762 --> 00:00:09.523
The catch is privacy.

00:00:09.523 --> 00:00:13.285
Google could check every link you click against its blocklist, but then Google would see every site you visit.

00:00:13.285 --> 00:00:17.046
And the full blocklist is far too big to ship to your phone.

00:00:17.046 --> 00:00:20.808
So how do you warn everyone, without watching everyone?

00:00:20.808 --> 00:00:25.816
Google's blocklist of bad sites is eight gigabytes, far too big for a phone.

00:00:25.816 --> 00:00:30.824
So Chrome ships a Bloom filter of it instead, a megabyte filter small enough for every device.

00:00:30.824 --> 00:00:35.831
You open a site.

00:00:35.831 --> 00:00:40.839
If the filter says not in the set, the answer is safe, and nothing leaves your phone.

00:00:40.839 --> 00:00:45.847
If it says maybe, Chrome sends only a short prefix of the hash to Google.

00:00:45.847 --> 00:00:50.855
Google sends back every bad hash with that prefix, and your phone finishes the check itself.

00:00:50.855 --> 00:00:53.659
Walk the three constraints.

00:00:53.659 --> 00:00:56.462
Scale.

00:00:56.462 --> 00:00:59.266
A hundred million bad sites is eight gigabytes, and you cannot push that to every phone.

00:00:59.266 --> 00:01:02.070
Privacy.

00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:04.874
Pinging Google on every link leaks your whole history.

00:01:04.874 --> 00:01:07.677
And load.

00:01:07.677 --> 00:01:10.481
Three billion users times every click is trillions of requests a day.

00:01:10.481 --> 00:01:13.285
A plain database loses on at least one of the three.

00:01:13.285 --> 00:01:18.567
The Bloom filter breaks the tie.

00:01:18.567 --> 00:01:23.850
Instead of the eight gigabyte list, each device downloads about a hundred and twenty-five megabytes, roughly sixty-four times smaller.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:29.132
It answers is this site bad right on the phone, in constant time, with no network call for the common case.

00:01:29.132 --> 00:01:34.415
Most sites are safe, so most checks end right there.

00:01:34.415 --> 00:01:39.005
The privacy comes from the filter's one-sided error.

00:01:39.005 --> 00:01:43.595
A no is certain, so a safe site never touches the network, and Google learns nothing.

00:01:43.595 --> 00:01:48.185
A maybe might be a false alarm, so the phone has to confirm.

00:01:48.185 --> 00:01:52.774
But it sends only a short hash prefix, one shared by many sites, not the page you opened.

00:01:52.774 --> 00:01:57.364
Google answers for the whole prefix, and the phone finishes locally.

00:01:57.364 --> 00:02:01.954
Google sees a crowd, never you.

00:02:01.954 --> 00:02:05.956
Line up the three options.

00:02:05.956 --> 00:02:09.957
Download the full database, and you ship eight gigabytes to every phone.

00:02:09.957 --> 00:02:13.958
Ask the cloud every time, and Google sees all of your browsing.

00:02:13.958 --> 00:02:17.960
The Bloom filter is the middle path.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.962
A small download, an instant local answer, and a hashed prefix only on a maybe.

00:02:21.962 --> 00:02:25.963
It gives up a tiny false-alarm rate, and buys both scale and privacy.

00:02:25.963 --> 00:02:31.118
A small filter on every device answers safe locally for almost all browsing, and only the rare maybe sends a prefix to Google.

00:02:31.118 --> 00:02:36.272
So a hundred million bad sites and three billion users cost almost no bandwidth, and leak almost nothing.

00:02:36.272 --> 00:02:41.427
That is the Bloom filter trade.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:46.582
A little error, for an enormous saving.
